former San Antonio mayor Julian Castro  emerged onto the national political  stage when he delivered the keynote  speech over here at the 2012 Democratic  convention he went on to serve as US  Secretary of Housing and Urban  Development under President Obama Castro  who was considered a rising star in the  Democratic Party shares his life story  in a new memoir it's called an unlikely  journey waking up from my American dream  his book comes out amid speculation that  he will in fact run for president in  2020 who Leon Castro joins us at the  table hello rising star it's good to be  with you Julian Castro it says there's  speculation because of your words you  did an interview with Rolling Stone and  you said I'll make a final decision  after November but I'm inclined to do it  so what is it going to take for you to  make the decision that you're going to  run well you know between now and  November 6 what I'm doing is helping  candidates that are in the 2018 ballot  but as I do that I'm also getting a  chance to travel around the country and  get a sense of what people are thinking  yeah I've heard very clearly that  there's a lot of dissatisfaction both  with this Congress and also with the  president I believe that people are  looking for a new generation of  leadership they're looking for new faces  and so I'm gonna continue to help folks  that are running before November 6  testing the waters for yourself though  well getting a sense of where people are  at sure  yeah I've been straightforward with  folks if people often play coy about  whether they're thinking about it or not  I said yeah I'm seriously thinking about  it and I'm likely to do it but between  now and November 6 I'm gonna focus on  the people that are on the ballot what  does new generation of leadership really  mean when it's at home though that's a  buzzword we hear a lot what does that  actually mean I think folks that are new  to Washington that bring a perspective  from the outside new faces you know you  always need a good mix in leadership you  need folks with many years of experience  to provide guidance but also folks that  are newer to it and the thing is whether  it's Andrew Gilliam or Stacey Abrams or  in my home state of Texas Bethel Rourke  we have these tremendous new faces in  2018 in the Democratic Party that are  offering strong leadership  that seems to be an implicit criticism  of Joe Biden who may run Mike Bloomberg  who may run the leader of the House of  Representatives Nancy Pelosi who may  become the speaker is that a criticism  of that older generation I think  everybody has their strengths and part  of the 20/20 process is going to be  you're probably going to have you know  15 to 20 people running at the beginning  right I view that as cathartic after  what the Democratic Party went through  in 2016  getting all those folks up there a lot  of talented people some some who have  many years of experience others that are  relatively new and when we get through  that and you get through more debates  and people feel like they've heard their  say from different parts of the  political spectrum yeah I think that  we're gonna be in a stronger position to  defeat Donald Trump in 2020 because of  that you mentioned better or roark  you're sitting among two Texans here nor  myself and and we watched I watched the  debate last night but our work opened it  up speaking Spanish but there is real  concern among Democrats that you're not  going to see a big Latin Latino turnout  in 2018 are you concerned about that oh  you know of course I'm concerned about  it because the numbers have shown  whether it's in midterm elections or  presidential elections that the rate of  Latino voting is not what it should be  and I think what it's going to take to  change that is that it can't be done you  know the six months before an election  or by any one or two candidate it's  going to take folks investing in a  massive and sustained effort to get  Latinos registered and then to turn out  to vote and until that happens I don't  think that the numbers are going to move  that much you write in your book you  talk about your mother having that  passion and being very politically  active it's you kind of what do you need  to do personally and Democrats need to  do to spark that enthusiasm among voters  the mace of this one out well I think  that that Bethel and O'Rourke and Stacey  Abrams and Andrew Gilliam are good  examples of folks that are speaking  directly to the things people care about  you know getting health insurance making  sure that your child can get a good  education talking about improving  neighborhoods and investing in  infrastructure you need to speak to  people where they are  and I think what often gets lost this is  that in the back-and-forth and in DC if  people just throw up their hands and  I've never had so many people tell me  that they stop watching cable news  because they just don't want to hear it  anymore exactly but what about the tone  of the race you know Michelle Obama said  a very classy tone when they go low we  go high  we have prominent Democrats like Eric  Holder who may also be running who says  when they go low we kick them how do you  you have the president of united states  calling a woman horse face where do you  stand on the tone of the title that that  is being said today in this country I  think that we need to call Donald Trump  out and and other folks out who are  uncivil does Eric Holder at the point  you know I'll let the Attorney General  Holder kind of explain what he means but  I would just say you need to be able to  stand up to folks and call them out but  at the same time history teaches us that  especially for Democrats that the ones  who who are ultimately victorious and  who will make change are people that had  a hopeful optimistic vision for the  future that was Barack Obama that was  John Kennedy and at the time that was  Bill Clinton and Jimmy Carter and and so  of course things do change in our  society and in many ways with the media  environment we have it's different but I  don't think that's changed all right  well mr. mayor full disclosure same age  we were in rival high schools Antonio go  MacArthur Brahmas yeah you look a lot  younger than I do  thank you friend an unlikely journey is  on sale now
 

